  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nCurrently, a trough is centered over the southern Canadian Rockies \nand northern US Rockies with a well defined short wave extending \nover the Four-Corners region. Strong southwesterly flow is present \nover the Southern and Central Plains. At the surface, an elongated \nsurface trough extends from the Upper Midwest into the Central High \nPlains with a cold front stretching along the extent of this \nfeature. Southerly winds remain breezy across the region, gusting \nbetween 20-30 mph at times. Above normal temperatures are expected \nagain this afternoon with highs reaching into the 60s across central \nKansas before the cold front arrives. Temperatures will approach 70 \nfor south-central and southeast Kansas.\n\nFor this afternoon and into the evening hours, as the synoptic \ntrough traverses the Northern Plains, the mid/upper level short wave \nwill lift across the Central Plains by late day. An expansive low \npressure system and associated cold front will pass across the \nstate. Ahead of this system, a plume of Gulf moisture will stretch \nacross the Southern and Central Plains. The anomalous warmth and \nmoisture combined with the potent frontal boundary will lead to \nwidespread rain and thunderstorm development across the forecast \narea by mid afternoon. Despite the time of year, a few of these \nstorms have the potential to produce some gusty winds and small \nhail, primarily across south-central Kansas where MUCAPE values \ncould reach 500 J/kg. Widespread severe weather is not expected.\n\nBehind the front for Sunday through the end of the forecast period, \nhigh pressure will build into the region. Much cooler conditions \nwill also settle in over the area with highs each afternoon through \nWednesday reaching into the 40s and overnight lows dipping into the \n20s and 30s. A short wave will sweep across the region on Tuesday. \nBut given the dry air mass and dominant high pressure, minimal \nimpacts are expected. Cloudy skies and very light precipitation will \nbe possible across the western half of the state. Only trace amounts \nof precip are expected at this time. Ridging will begin to build in \nover the Plains for the end of the week, leading to a slight warmup \nfor the end of the forecast period. Afternoon highs for Thursday and \nFriday will reach to around 50 degrees. Overall, dry conditions and \nmostly clear skies are expected for the Sun-Fri time frame.",
